```

## Simple Test Example

A basic NUnit test using WebApplicationFactory looks like this:

```csharp
[TestFixture]
public class MyApiTests
{
    [Test]
    public async Task Get_EndpointReturnsSuccessAndCorrectContentType()
    {
        // Arrange
        var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>().WithWebHostBuilder(builder =>
        {
            builder.UseEnvironment("Testing");
        });
        var client = factory.CreateClient();

        // Act
        HttpResponseMessage response = await client.GetAsync("api/values");

        // Assert
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        Assert.AreEqual("application/json; charset=utf-8", 
            response.Content.Headers.ContentType.ToString());
    }
}
```

## Base Test Class Pattern

Many examples show creating a base test class to encapsulate the WebApplicationFactory setup and provide a clean interface for derived tests:

```csharp
public class CustomBaseTest
{
    private readonly CustomWebApplicationFactory _webApplicationFactory;
    public ExternalServicesMock ExternalServicesMock { get; }

    public CustomBaseTest()
    {
        ExternalServicesMock = new ExternalServicesMock();
        _webApplicationFactory = new CustomWebApplicationFactory(ExternalServicesMock);
    }

    public HttpClient GetClient() => _webApplicationFactory.CreateClient();
}
```

## NUnit Lifecycle Management

With NUnit, TestServer and HttpClient are disposed when NUnit disposes of the WebApplicationFactory in the TearDown method. You can optimize this by instantiating WebApplicationFactory once using NUnit's test lifecycle methods.

## Required NuGet Packages

For NUnit integration testing with WebApplicationFactory, you need these packages:
- `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing` - Provides the WebApplicationFactory class to streamline bootstrapping the SUT with TestServer
- `NUnit` - The testing framework itself
- `Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk` - Required for running tests from Visual Studio
- `NUnit3TestAdapter` - Needed to run NUnit tests from Visual Studio test explorer

## Minimal API Considerations

When using Minimal APIs, you may encounter issues with missing testhost.deps.json due to accessing the wrong Program class, since the generated Minimal API Program class is internal. You can fix this by declaring a partial Program class to make it public.

These examples demonstrate that WebApplicationFactory is agnostic to the testing framework you choose and works well with NUnit, providing a solid foundation for integration testing ASP.NET Core applications.

